<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="α-amyrin biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5377" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Arabidopsis thaliana col. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; Thousands of nonsteroidal triterpenes have been identified, though functions for specific compounds remain to be determined. The triterpene &amp;alpha;-amyrin is synthesized from oxidosqualene by a multifunctional 2,3-oxidosqualene-triterpene cyclase, |FRAME: AT1G78960-MONOMER|. This enzyme catalyzes the cyclization, rearrangement and deprotonation of oxidosqualene to simultaneously form the triterpene compounds &amp;alpha;-amyrin, &amp;beta;-amyrin, and lupeol |CITS:[11247608]|. In addition, the only other mixed &amp;alpha;/&amp;beta;-amyrin synthase identified so far, i.e. catalyzing both |FRAME:CPD-8250| and |FRAME:CPD-6948| from |FRAME:EPOXYSQUALENE| in a ratio of 2.5 : 1 and therefore involved in the two triterpenoid pathways |FRAME:PWY-7068| and |FRAME:PWY-7069| has been characterized from the leaf epidermome of &lt;i&gt;Catharanthus roseus&lt;/i&gt; |CITS:[22837051]|.</Description>
  <Comment source="Automated Conversion">This GPML file was automatically generated from PlantCyc flatfiles.</Comment>
